  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Mid November warm and dry spell ends Sunday/Monday\n\nHigh clouds increase from south to north throughout the day\nwhich keeps max temps a few degrees cooler vs. Friday, but \nstill +10-20 degrees above daily climo. Rain chances increase \non Sunday as a complex low pressure system moves up the East \nCoast. There remains significant uncertainty in recent model \nguidance regarding how far west the rainfall can expand on the\nnorthwest side of the low track. Highest odds for rain remain\nfocused across the eastern portion of CPA Sunday into Sunday\nnight, in an area of 700 hPa fgen forcing. Deterministic QPF\nranges from a few hundredths along I-99 to about 0.40\" in the\nlower Susq valley, but PoPs are still only in the 30-60 pct\nrange given low-medium confidence. It is worth noting that \nthere are an increasing number of ensemble members that keep all\nof central PA dry, in part due to earlier phasing of the upper \ntrough with a a stronger offshore low, resulting in the 700 hPa \nfgen forcing being placed well to our east. \n\nA cold front will move in Sunday night into early Monday behind\nthe departing coastal low. This could generate a few rain\nshowers, particularly in the Laurel Highlands into early Monday\nmorning. Otherwise expect decreasing clouds through the day\nMonday.\n-----------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Colder with snow showers possible in the \nnorthern tier Monday Night, followed by potential rain and/or \nsnow with a coastal storm on Thanksgiving\n\nThe core of the cold airmass will move in Monday night into\nTuesday. Snow showers will be possible mainly in the northern \ntier Monday night as the trough axis swings through. We have \nhigh confidence in a noticeable cool down on Tuesday with fcst \nhighs 15 to 25 degrees lower than this weekend. Next week will \ncertainly feel more like late November with colder, more \nseasonal temperatures Tuesday through the Thanksgiving holiday \nweekend. \n\nThere appears to be a general consensus toward increasing precip\npotential from mid to late week including on Thanksgiving Day.\nAt this point, Probabilistic WSSI (Winter Storm Severity Index)\nsuggests about a 20 pct chance of minor winter weather impacts \nin the northern tier (mainly higher elevations) on \nThanksgiving. Prob of 0.50\" of QPF range from 30% in the NW to \n50% in eastern zones. Most model guidance shows the antecedent \nhigh retreating rapidly as the coastal storm moves north, which\nwould make it difficult to keep cold air in place long enough \nto create anything more than minor winter weather impacts, but \nthere is still plenty of time for the forecast to change. Lake \neffect snow and rain showers will be possible on Friday behind \nthe departing low as NW flows blows across the GLakes.",
